**user**:
> I have a set of risks included in a study which are based on expert elicitation estimates, rather than detailed process models. I have been calling these "unmodelled" risks, but I have gotten the objection that this is unclear. Do you have other suggestions I could consider?
**assistant**:
> Here are some options, grouped by nuance. You can also pair a short label with a clarifying phrase the first time you use it.
> 
> ### Emphasizing *how* they’re quantified (expert-based)
> 
> - **Expert-elicited risks**  
> - **Elicitation-based risks**  
> - **Expert-judgment risks**  
> - **Expert-assessed risks**
> 
> These are clearest if the key distinction is “not process-model-based, but still quantitatively assessed.”
> 
> ### Emphasizing the *absence of process-based models*
> 
> - **Non–process-modelled risks**  
> - **Non–mechanistically modelled risks**  
> - **Non–system-modelled risks**  
> - **Off-model risks** (colloquial, but sometimes effective if defined)
> 
> You might write, for example:  
> > “…a set of non–process-modelled (expert-elicited) risks…”
> 
> ### Emphasizing that they’re outside the *core* modelling framework
> 
> - **Externally quantified risks**  
> - **Supplementary (expert-elicited) risks**  
> - **Add-on risks estimated via expert elicitation**  
> - **Out-of-framework risks** (if you define your “framework” clearly)
> 
> ### Emphasizing they’re not simulated but still structured
> 
> - **Non-simulated risks, quantified by expert elicitation**  
> - **Non-simulation-based risks**  
> - **Empirically/experientially assessed risks** (if experts are drawing on empirical literatures and experience)
> 
> ---
> 
> If you want a single, precise term in a formal paper, my top candidates would be:
> 
> - **“Expert-elicited risks”** (most transparent and neutral)  
> - **“Non–process-modelled (expert-elicited) risks”** (very clear contrast with process-based risks)
> 
> You could define them once as, for example:
> 
> > “In addition to process-based impact categories, we incorporate a set of non–process-modelled, expert-elicited risks—categories for which no detailed process model is available, and which are instead quantified using structured expert judgment.”
> 
> If you share the sentence/paragraph where “unmodelled risks” currently appears, I can propose tailored replacements that fit your tone and context.
